        ":question/id": "f595ef5e-144e-5941-a10d-4dcd435568ad",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $x^2-7x$.\n\nHalve the linear coefficient, then add and subtract the square of that half. Fill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$x^2-7x=(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^2-$ {{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{7}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-7x&=\\underbrace{x^2-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{49}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{49}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-7x$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{7}{2}$ and $\\frac{49}{4}$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied.",
